  "responses": {
    "_comment": "Empty until a real sandbox run. Recording a fabricated acknowledgement here would make the conformance test assert that our own guess is stable, which is worse than asserting nothing."
  },
  "scenarios": {
    "crosses_midnight": "An overnight shift ends on a different calendar day from the one it started. `date_of_service` is derived from the start, and an aggregator that reads the end date instead will file the visit against the wrong day \u2014 which surfaces as a duplicate or an unauthorised visit, never as an error message.",
    "manual_exception": "The phone was dead and a coordinator entered the visit afterwards. Every state treats manual entry as auditable rather than forbidden, and the reason has to survive the mapping \u2014 a manual visit transmitted as though it were GPS-captured is a misrepresentation, not a rounding error.",
    "normal_mobile_gps": "The ordinary case, and the baseline every other scenario is a deviation from. If this one is wrong, nothing else matters.",
    "telephony_no_coordinates": "A caregiver with no smartphone clocks in from the client's landline, so there are no coordinates at all. `06_Compliance...` Section 1 accepts telephony as a capture method; a map that requires a latitude will reject every visit from this workforce, and that workforce is not small."
  }
}
